cartridge "ALIEN ARMADA/J.MATIAS/1985"

' ALIEN ARMADA
' A ColecoVision game based on "Alien Armada" by John Matias, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette May 1985 (Commodore 64 machine language, and a VIC-20 version).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules are the original's (read from the
' C64 machine code, orig/armada64.dis, and the VIC-20 version's
' instructions, orig/vic-intro.txt), rebuilt for the ColecoVision: smooth
' sprites, a Gorg mothership and a wall of guard droids in tiles, sound.
'
' The game: you are a Zargian star warrior. Above you the Gorg mothership
' hides behind four rows of guard droids; each droid takes three hits
' (whole, dented, cracked, gone). Dig a hole through the wall under the
' mothership and hit it with one laser shot. Meanwhile a bomb drops at the
' spot where you stand, a bird swoops down and four aliens home in on you.
' Whatever lands on your ship costs a ship. From 10,000 points a smartbomb
' joins them: it homes in fast and your laser cannot stop it.
'
' From the original (orig/armada64.dis): one game step moves the ship one
' column, the laser one row, the bomb one row, the bird one row every 4th
' step and the four aliens one row (and one column toward you) every 5th
' step; the step's delay loop by level 0-9 (DATA 50,45,40,35,30,35,30,25,
' 20,15 x 256 loops): worked out as steps a second, about 15 at level 0;
' levels 0-4 give 5 ships, 5-9 give 3; a new bomb starts above you, birds,
' aliens and the smartbomb at a random column; four rows of droids that
' take three hits; the laser passes over the bomb, which falls on; points (shown with a fixed last 0): bomb 10, bird 100,
' alien 50, droid 50 a hit, mothership 5000 (90 x 5 + 50 in the code);
' hitting the mothership (its four columns) starts a new wave with a new
' wall and the ship at the left; something landing in your column costs a
' ship and play goes on; the smartbomb (object 7) homes in one column and
' one row a step and absorbs laser shots; the high score.
'
' Liberties: the field is 28 columns (the C64's 40) and 11 rows between the
' wall and the ship (the C64's 18): every speed keeps the original's
' crossing times, smoothly in 1/32 pixels; the faster levels are eased
' (level 9 is 2.2 x level 0, the C64's about 3 x) so they stay playable by
' a person; the three aliens after the first come in 20 pixels apart at the
' start of a wave (the C64 started all four on one row, where they stay
' stacked as they home in); the laser passes over a bomb as on the C64
' and scores it once a shot (the C64 scored it in each cell where they
' met); the smartbomb gives no points (the C64 counted its absorbed shots as a droid
' hit) and comes in from 10,000 points on (the C64 checked only scores of
' 10,000 to 10,990); a pause of 3/4 second after a lost ship and 1 1/2
' after the mothership (the C64 played its sound in a busy loop); level
' the smartbomb homes in at 3/4 of the ship's speed (the C64's moved
' one column a step like the ship, so it could not be outrun); level
' chosen on the title with the stick (the C64 asked for a key 0-9); GAME
' OVER then the title (the C64 asked to press space); no shift-key pause.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the ship left and right,
' either button fires (hold it to keep firing). On the title, up and down
' choose, left and right change the level, a button chooses (START,
' HOW TO PLAY: two pages, a button turns the page, 30 s idle goes back;
' CREDITS).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. Own xorshift random numbers.

